../ml4t-data/docs/dukascopy_node_manual.md:1:# Dukascopy Node Documentation Manual
../ml4t-data/docs/dukascopy_node_manual.md:7: dukascopy-node – is an open-source (opens in a new tab) CLI tool and Node.js module for downloading historical market tick data from Dukascopy (opens in a new tab) . 
../ml4t-data/docs/dukascopy_node_manual.md:286: In order to download historical price data, dukascopy-node needs to generate a list of URLs of all artifacts. These artifacts will be then downloaded from Dukascopy datafeed servers, decompressed, parsed and aggregated. 
../ml4t-data/docs/dukascopy_node_manual.md:287: In some cases such list of URLs can be huge. Since we do not want to overwhelm Dukascopy servers with a lot of requests at the same time, and in order not to fall under the rate-limiting restrictions, we need to break this list down into groups (a.k.a. batches) and download them one-by-one with a pause in between. 
../ml4t-data/docs/dukascopy_node_manual.md:572: getRealTimeRates is a function that fetches real-time financial market data from Dukascopy&#x27;s free server API. Unlike getHistoricalRates which retrieves archived tick/candle data from Dukascopy&#x27;s historical database, this function accesses live market data feeds. 
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:1:# ADR 0002: Dukascopy ingestion and publication boundary
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:8:The existing Dukascopy estate mixes provider-specific retrieval, timestamp interpretation, custom BI5 decoding, Python subprocess wrappers, direct Parquet writes, and inconsistent completeness assumptions. The rebuild needs one source-facing implementation and one repository publication authority.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:12:1. TypeScript/Node.js owns Dukascopy-specific retrieval and normalization through the `dukascopy-node` API. It owns source batching, retries, cache use, Dukascopy timestamp interpretation, requested timeframe, and bid-side OHLCV normalization.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:13:2. Provider-specific Python decoding or reinterpretation of Dukascopy data is not part of the new ingestion path.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:15:4. Canonical Dukascopy OHLCV uses bid prices. Spread-sensitive or bid/ask research uses distinct datasets rather than changing the meaning of the canonical OHLCV series.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0002-dukascopy-ingestion-and-publication-boundary.md:27:### Keep Python as the Dukascopy interpretation layer
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0004-dukascopy-record-eligibility-and-cutover-contract.md:1:# ADR 0004: Dukascopy record, eligibility, and cutover contract
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0004-dukascopy-record-eligibility-and-cutover-contract.md:8:The greenfield Dukascopy rebuild now has a settled source boundary, staging model, and provenance policy. The remaining choices concern the exact canonical record shape, volume units, historical availability, eligibility semantics, and cutover behavior. These decisions affect every rebuilt instrument and every downstream consumer.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0004-dukascopy-record-eligibility-and-cutover-contract.md:12:1. The Arrow handoff schema for canonical Dukascopy OHLCV records is:
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0004-dukascopy-record-eligibility-and-cutover-contract.md:21:3. Dukascopy retrieval explicitly requests `volumes = true` and `volumeUnits = units`. The canonical `volume` field preserves provider-reported volume in those units. It is not relabeled as centralized exchange-traded volume for OTC FX instruments.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0001-canonical-dukascopy-rebuild.md:1:# ADR 0001: Canonical Dukascopy rebuild
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0001-canonical-dukascopy-rebuild.md:8:Existing Dukascopy-derived stores contain inconsistent timestamp schemas, incomplete partitions, and cadence mismatches. Multiple ingestion paths encode different assumptions. The current upstream ML4T Data contract differs from several local historical conventions.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0001-canonical-dukascopy-rebuild.md:12:1. Current upstream ML4T Data semantics are authoritative for new Dukascopy datasets.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0001-canonical-dukascopy-rebuild.md:13:2. Dukascopy-derived datasets will be rebuilt in parallel rather than repaired in place.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0001-canonical-dukascopy-rebuild.md:16:5. The replacement path is greenfield-first: legacy Dukascopy scripts, schemas, and compatibility behavior are not design constraints. Reuse is allowed only when a component independently satisfies the new source and ML4T contracts.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0003-dukascopy-handoff-provenance-and-consumer-cutover.md:1:# ADR 0003: Dukascopy handoff, provenance, and consumer cutover
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0003-dukascopy-handoff-provenance-and-consumer-cutover.md:14:3. Rebuild all desired Dukascopy-derived datasets from source rather than selectively repairing known-bad stores. Execute in independently acceptable waves: FX majors and USDJPY, FX crosses, metals, then index and commodity CFDs.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0003-dukascopy-handoff-provenance-and-consumer-cutover.md:20:9. Material unexplained disagreement with Dukascopy source semantics or independent source evidence blocks acceptance.
../ml4t-data/docs/adr/0003-dukascopy-handoff-provenance-and-consumer-cutover.md:33:Rejected. Existing failures came from shared ingestion design defects, so absence of an observed failure is not evidence that another legacy Dukascopy store is trustworthy.
